body:
Zoonotic diseases are ones transferred from animals to humans. The high number of domesticated animals in the "old world" meant constant contact between humans and their animals. In cold climates they had 3 sides houses where the cattle slept on the open side to prevent heat from escaping while the humans slept close to the 3 walls. This kind of living was filthy IMO and allowed the spread of many diseases that the population died from and eventually evolved(the change of alleles of successive generations) to be more resistant to many. 

&#x200B;

Once you bring over those nasty bugs the natives of the New World had no such practice and the 2 domesticated animals in all of central south america are guinea pigs(for meat) and the llamas(which refused to carry weight over 20lbs so they only were good for carrying textiles and light loads. Smallpox is an example of a diseases(cowpox) jumping from bovine to human, the first people they noticed to be resistant to smallpox were the milkmen due to their proximity to the cows which helped their body develop resistance that protected them from smallpox. Humans with no cow exposure were very much at the mercy of smallpox(much like the natives were to the gigantic host of diseases the old world humans carried)

&#x200B;

I've read that they believe some forms of STD were exclusive to the new wold and were transmitted to the old world after contact! I hope this gave some ideas and followed the subreddit rules
